# Embedded System Glossary

Key terms and definitions used across embedded firmware, BSP, RTOS, and hardware bring-up — explained by Qmax Systems engineers.

## A/D converter
A device that converts an analog signal to a digital number. See also D/A converter.

## ABEL
A hardware description language (HDL) used mostly to program CPLDs.

## Active Low
A signal that is asserted when it is in the low state. Using * or # or ! to denote low-asserted signals.

## Address Bus
A set of signals used by a processor to identify the memory location or I/O device that is to be read or written.

## Aliasing
A distortion of the reconstructed signal that occurs when the sampling rate is less than twice the highest frequency content of the signal being sampled. See also Nyquist sampling criterion.

## Analog
Characterized by a continuous range of values; not discrete. The opposite of digital.

## Anode
The positive terminal of a battery or the p side of a diode.

## Aperiodic
Describes a task that does not execute on a regular basis.

## Aperiodic Server
An RTOS feature that allows a periodic task to serve aperiodic requests. Using aperiodic server means that schedulability analysis is still valid for the periodic tasks.

## Ariane 5
A European Space Agency rocket that was lost because of an inertial reference system software error (a 64-bit floating point number was truncated to a 16-bit integer).

## ARM
A popular 32-bit processor core found in many embedded systems.

## ARM Thumb
An operating mode supported by ARM processors in which instructions are 16 bits wide. Thumb mode gives smaller code size than regular ARM mode.

## ARP
The Address Resolution Protocol. A low-level Internet networking protocol used to determine the hardware (MAC) address associated with a given IP address.

## Assembler
A program that translates assembly language to object code.

## Assembly Language
A low-level programming language in which each statement corresponds to a single machine instruction.

## Asynchronous Communications
Serial communications in which each character is sent individually, with start and stop bits to frame each character. Contrast with synchronous communications.

## Atomic
An operation that is not divisible. Either the complete operation executes, or none of it does.

## ATVEF
Advanced Television Enhancement Forum. A group that created a specification for enhanced TV.

## Average-case Execution Time
The average time it takes a piece of code to execute. Contrast with WCET.

## BASIC Stamp
A small microcontroller module from Parallax, Inc. that contains a PIC microcontroller running a BASIC interpreter in ROM.

## Bi-directional
A signal that can carry information in either direction. Also called bidirectional.

## Big-endian
A byte ordering scheme in which, for a multibyte value, the most significant byte is stored at the lowest memory address. Contrast with little-endian.

## Binary Semaphore
A semaphore that can only take values 0 and 1. Used for signaling between tasks. Contrast with counting semaphore.

## Board Support Package
Code that provides software access to the features of a particular circuit board.

## Bond-out Processor
A processor that has additional pins used for debugging, typically using JTAG. See ICE.

## Breakpoint
A location in code at which program execution is halted so the programmer can examine the state of the system.

## BSP
See Board Support Package.

## Busicom
A Japanese calculator company. In 1969, they hired Ted Hoff of Intel to develop chips for their calculators. This led to the creation of the Intel 4004, the world's first microprocessor.

## Busy Wait
A form of synchronization in which the processor repeatedly checks a register or memory location until the desired value is present. Also called a tight loop, spin loop, or polling. Busy waits waste processor time.

## C99
The 1999 ANSI/ISO standard for the C language.

## Central Processing Unit
The part of a computer that executes instructions. Also called a processor or microprocessor.

## Checksum
A value calculated from the data in a message or memory block that can be used to detect errors. Simpler but less powerful than a CRC.

## Compiler
A program that translates from a high-level language to object code.

## Complex Instruction Set Computer
A processor design philosophy in which the instruction set is large and includes complex instructions. x86 architecture is CISC. Contrast with RISC.

## Complex Programmable Logic Device
A large programmable logic device that can be used to implement complex digital logic.

## Context
All the information needed to resume execution of a task or interrupt after a context switch. Includes register values and other state information.

## Context Switch
The act of saving the context of one task and restoring the context of another. Allows multiple tasks to share a processor.

## Counter/Timer
Hardware that counts pulses or measures time. Available on most microcontrollers.

## Counting Semaphore
A semaphore that can take any non-negative value. Used to protect shared resources. Contrast with binary semaphore.

## CRC
Cyclic Redundancy Check. A type of checksum that can detect multiple bit errors. More powerful but more complex than a simple checksum.

## Critical Section
A section of code that must not be interrupted. Often protected by disabling interrupts.

## Cross-Compiler
A compiler that runs on one type of processor but generates code for a different type of processor.

## D/A converter
A device that converts a digital number to an analog signal. See also A/D converter.

## Data Bus
A set of signals used to carry data between a processor and memory or I/O devices.

## Deadline
In a real-time system, the time by which a task must complete its execution.

## Deadlock
A situation where two or more tasks are each waiting for the other to release a resource, causing both tasks to be stuck indefinitely.

## Debug Monitor
Firmware that runs on the target system and communicates with a debugger running on the host. Allows the host debugger to examine and modify the target's state.

## Debugger
A tool that allows a programmer to examine the state of a program during execution, set breakpoints, and single-step through code.

## Device Driver
Software that provides a standard interface to a hardware device, hiding the hardware-specific details.

## Device Programmer
A tool used to program ROM, Flash, or other memory devices.

## Digital Filter
A filter implemented in software or digital hardware. Contrast with analog filter.

## Digital Signal Processor
A processor optimized for performing mathematical operations on digital signals. Often has special instructions for multiply-accumulate operations.

## Direct Memory Access
A technique in which a device transfers data directly to or from memory without involving the processor.

## Down Counter
A counter that decrements from an initial value to zero.

## DRAM
Dynamic Random Access Memory. RAM that requires periodic refreshing to maintain its contents.

## DSP
See Digital Signal Processor.

## EEPROM
Electrically Erasable Programmable Read-Only Memory. Non-volatile memory that can be erased and reprogrammed electrically.

## Electromagnetic Spectrum
The range of all possible frequencies of electromagnetic radiation. Includes radio waves, microwaves, infrared, visible light, ultraviolet, X-rays, and gamma rays.

## Embedded C++
A subset of C++ designed for use in embedded systems. Excludes features that are not suitable for embedded development.

## Embedded System
A computer system designed to perform a dedicated function. Unlike a general-purpose computer, an embedded system is usually designed for a specific application.

## Emulator
A hardware device that allows a processor to be substituted for a target processor during development. Allows debugging of code running in the actual target hardware. See also ICE.

## Endianness
The byte ordering scheme used by a processor or system. See big-endian and little-endian.

## EPROM
Erasable Programmable Read-Only Memory. Non-volatile memory that can be erased by exposure to ultraviolet light and reprogrammed.

## Executable
A program in a form that can be directly executed by a processor. The result of linking one or more object files.

## Field-Programmable Gate Array
A programmable logic device that can implement complex digital logic. FPGAs contain many programmable logic cells that can be connected in different ways.

## Firmware
Software stored in non-volatile memory. Often used to refer to the software in an embedded system.

## Flash
Non-volatile memory that can be erased and reprogrammed in-circuit. Flash memory is commonly used to store firmware in embedded systems.

## Forth
A programming language with a simple, extensible syntax based on a postfix stack.

## Fuzzy Logic
A form of logic that allows for degrees of truth, rather than simple true/false values. Used in some control systems.

## Geartooth Hall Effect Sensor
A sensor that uses the Hall effect to detect the teeth of a rotating gear. Used to measure rotational speed or position.

## General-Purpose Computer
A computer designed to run a wide variety of programs. Contrast with an embedded system, which is designed for a specific application.

## General-Purpose I/O
A pin on a microcontroller that can be configured as an input or output and controlled directly by software.

## Glitch
A brief, unwanted pulse on a signal. Can cause incorrect operation of digital circuits.

## Glue Logic
Simple logic circuits (gates, flip-flops, etc.) used to connect other components together. Often implemented in a PLD or FPGA.

## GNU Tools
A collection of free software development tools including the GCC compiler, GDB debugger, and many others. Widely used for embedded development.

## Gray Code
A binary code in which successive values differ in only one bit. Used in encoders and other applications where error detection is important.

## Heap
A region of memory used for dynamic memory allocation. Memory is allocated from the heap using malloc() and freed using free().

## High-level Language
A programming language that is more abstract than assembly language. C, C++, and Ada are high-level languages used in embedded development.

## Host
The computer used to develop software for an embedded system. Contrast with target.

## I/O
Input/Output. The means by which a computer communicates with the outside world.

## I/O Device
A hardware device that provides input to or receives output from a processor.

## I/O Map
A memory map showing the addresses of I/O devices.

## I/O Space
A separate address space used for I/O devices on some processors. Contrast with memory-mapped I/O.

## I2C
Inter-Integrated Circuit. A two-wire serial bus standard commonly used to connect microcontrollers to sensors and other peripherals.

## ICE
In-Circuit Emulator. See Emulator.

## Instruction Pointer
A register that holds the address of the next instruction to be executed. Also called the program counter.

## Interrupt
A signal to the processor that causes it to stop what it is doing and execute an interrupt service routine.

## Interrupt Latency
The time between when an interrupt occurs and when the first instruction of the interrupt service routine is executed.

## Interrupt Service Routine
The code that executes when an interrupt occurs. Also called an interrupt handler.

## Interrupt Type
A number that identifies the source of an interrupt. Used to find the interrupt vector.

## Interrupt Vector
The address of an interrupt service routine.

## Interrupt Vector Table
A table of interrupt vectors.

## Intertask Communication
Mechanisms for exchanging data between tasks. Includes message queues, shared memory, and other techniques.

## Intertask Synchronization
Mechanisms for synchronizing the execution of tasks. Includes semaphores, mutexes, and events.

## Java Processor
A processor that can directly execute Java bytecode.

## Jitter
Variation in the timing of a signal. In real-time systems, jitter refers to variation in the time a task is started relative to its scheduled time.

## JTAG
Joint Test Action Group. A standard interface used for debugging and programming embedded systems.

## Jump Table
A table of function pointers used to dispatch function calls based on an index or key.

## Jumper
A small connector used to select between different hardware configurations.

## Junction Transistor
A transistor made from two p-n junctions. The most common type of transistor.

## Kernel
The core of an operating system. Provides basic services such as task scheduling, memory management, and I/O.

## Kernel Mode
A processor mode in which all instructions are available. Contrast with user mode.

## Kibi-
A prefix meaning 1,024. Used in computing to avoid confusion with the SI prefix kilo-, which means 1,000.

## Linker
A program that combines object files and libraries to create an executable.

## Lint
A program that analyzes C source code to detect errors and potential problems.

## Linux
An open-source operating system. Used in some embedded systems.

## Little-endian
A byte ordering scheme in which, for a multibyte value, the least significant byte is stored at the lowest memory address. Contrast with big-endian.

## Locator
A linker or linker script that assigns final addresses to code and data sections.

## Logic analyzer
A test instrument that captures and displays digital signals. Used to debug digital circuits and communication protocols.

## MAC
Media Access Control. The lower sublayer of the data link layer in the OSI model. Also, the hardware address of a network interface.

## MAC address
A unique identifier assigned to a network interface. Used at the MAC layer.

## Mebi-
A prefix meaning 1,048,576 (2^20). Used in computing to avoid confusion with the SI prefix mega-, which means 1,000,000.

## Memory Map
A diagram showing how memory addresses are assigned to different devices or memory regions in a system.

## Memory-mapped I/O
A technique in which I/O devices are accessed using the same instructions as memory. The devices appear at specific addresses in the memory map.

## Memory Space
The range of addresses that a processor can access.

## Microcontroller
A processor that includes memory and I/O peripherals on the same chip. Commonly used in embedded systems.

## Microprocessor
A processor implemented on a single chip. Contrast with microcontroller, which also includes memory and I/O.

## MISRA C
A set of guidelines for using C in safety-critical embedded systems. Published by the Motor Industry Software Reliability Association.

## Monitor
A simple debug interface that allows basic interaction with an embedded system via a serial port.

## Multiply-and-accumulate
An operation that multiplies two values and adds the result to an accumulator. Common in DSPs.

## Multiprocessing
Using more than one processor.

## Multitasking
A technique in which a processor rapidly switches between multiple tasks, giving the appearance of simultaneous execution.

## Mutex
Mutual exclusion semaphore. A synchronization primitive used to protect a shared resource from simultaneous access by multiple tasks.

## Mutual Exclusion
Ensuring that only one task at a time can access a shared resource.

## NAK
Negative Acknowledgment. A signal indicating that a data transmission was not received correctly. Contrast with ACK.

## Nanosecond
One billionth of a second. A common unit of time in embedded and digital systems.

## Netlist
A description of the connections between components in a circuit. Used as input to PCB layout tools.

## Network Processor
A processor optimized for processing network packets. Often includes special hardware for common networking operations.

## Nibble
A group of four bits.

## Nonmaskable Interrupt
An interrupt that cannot be disabled by software. Used for critical events such as power failure.

## NVRAM
Non-Volatile Random Access Memory. RAM that retains its contents when power is removed.

## Object Code
Machine code generated by a compiler or assembler.

## Object File
A file containing object code. One or more object files are combined by a linker to create an executable.

## On-chip Debug
A debugging interface built into the processor chip. Allows debugging without additional hardware. See also JTAG.

## One-time Programmable
Memory that can be programmed only once. Contrast with EPROM and Flash.

## Opcode
The part of a machine instruction that specifies the operation to be performed.

## Open Source
Software whose source code is freely available for anyone to use, modify, and distribute.

## Operating System
Software that manages computer hardware resources and provides common services for computer programs.

## Oscilloscope
A test instrument that displays electrical signals as a function of time.

## OTP
See One-time Programmable.

## Parallel Processing
Performing multiple computations simultaneously, either on a single processor with multiple execution units or on multiple processors.

## Peripheral
A device that is external to the processor core but connected to it. Examples include timers, UARTs, and ADCs.

## Physical Address
The actual address used to access memory or an I/O device. Contrast with virtual address.

## PIC
Peripheral Interface Controller. A family of popular microcontrollers from Microchip Technology.

## PID
Proportional-Integral-Derivative. A common control algorithm used in control systems.

## PLD
Programmable Logic Device. A device that can be programmed to implement custom digital logic.

## Polling
Checking the state of a device repeatedly in a loop. Contrast with interrupt-driven I/O.

## Preemptive
Describes a scheduler that can interrupt a running task to run a higher-priority task.

## Priority
In a real-time operating system, a number assigned to a task that determines its importance relative to other tasks.

## Priority Inversion
A situation where a high-priority task is blocked waiting for a resource held by a lower-priority task.

## Process
A program in execution. In some operating systems, processes have separate address spaces. Contrast with thread.

## Processor
The part of a computer that executes instructions. See also CPU, microprocessor, microcontroller.

## Processor Family
A group of processors from the same manufacturer that share a common instruction set architecture.

## Processor-Independent
Code that can be compiled and run on different processor architectures without modification.

## Processor-Specific
Code that is written for a particular processor and cannot be easily ported to other processors.

## Profiler
A tool that measures how much time is spent in each part of a program. Used to identify performance bottlenecks.

## Programmable Logic Device
See PLD.

## PROM
Programmable Read-Only Memory. Non-volatile memory that can be programmed once but not erased.

## Protocol Stack
The software that implements a communication protocol. Typically organized as a series of layers.

## Pulse Width Modulation
A technique for encoding information in the width of a pulse. Commonly used to control motors and generate analog signals.

## Quadrature Encoder
A type of encoder that uses two signals 90 degrees out of phase to detect both position and direction of rotation.

## Quantum Programming
A programming model that divides computation into small, fixed-length time slices.

## Race Condition
A situation where the behavior of a program depends on the relative timing of events. Can cause unpredictable behavior in multitasking systems.

## Random Access Memory
Memory that can be read and written at any location in any order. RAM loses its contents when power is removed.

## Rate Monotonic Analysis
A method for determining if a set of periodic tasks can be scheduled to meet their deadlines. Tasks with shorter periods are given higher priority.

## Read-only memory
Memory whose contents are fixed at manufacturing time or during programming. ROM retains its contents when power is removed.

## Real-time Operating System
An operating system designed to meet strict timing requirements. Tasks are scheduled based on priority and deadlines.

## Real-time System
A system in which the correctness of the system depends not only on the logical results but also on the time at which the results are produced.

## Recursive
A function that calls itself.

## Reentrant
A function that can be safely called by multiple tasks simultaneously.

## Register
A small, fast storage location within the processor. Registers are used to hold temporary values during computation.

## Relocatable
Code that can be loaded and executed at any memory address.

## Reset Address
The memory address at which the processor begins execution after reset.

## Reset Code
The first code executed by a processor after reset. Typically initializes the processor and sets up the C runtime environment.

## Reset Vector
The interrupt vector that contains the address of the reset code.

## Resistance Temperature Detector
A temperature sensor that uses the change in resistance of a metal with temperature. More accurate than a thermocouple or thermistor.

## RMA
Rate Monotonic Analysis. See Rate Monotonic Analysis.

## ROM Emulator
A device that plugs into the ROM socket and allows ROM contents to be quickly updated during development.

## Scheduler
The part of an operating system that decides which task should run next.

## Semaphore
A synchronization primitive used to control access to a shared resource or to signal between tasks.

## Simulator
A software tool that models the behavior of a target system on the host computer. Allows debugging without target hardware.

## Software Flow Control
A technique for controlling the flow of data in a serial communication link using special characters (XON/XOFF).

## Software Interrupt
An interrupt caused by software, not hardware. Used to implement system calls.

## SPI
Serial Peripheral Interface. A synchronous serial bus commonly used to connect microcontrollers to sensors, displays, and other peripherals.

## SRAM
Static Random Access Memory. RAM that does not require refreshing. Faster but more expensive than DRAM.

## Stack
A region of memory used to store local variables and return addresses. The stack grows and shrinks as functions are called and return.

## Stack Frame
The portion of the stack used by a single function call. Contains local variables, saved registers, and the return address.

## Startup Code
The code that runs immediately after reset, before main(). Initializes the processor, sets up the C runtime, and calls main().

## Target
The embedded system being developed. Contrast with host.

## Task
In an RTOS, a thread of execution that can be scheduled independently.

## Thermistor
A temperature sensor whose resistance changes significantly with temperature. Less accurate than an RTD but cheaper and simpler.

## Thermocouple
A temperature sensor made from two different metals joined together. Generates a small voltage proportional to temperature.

## Thread
A thread of execution within a process. Threads within the same process share an address space.

## Timer
Hardware that measures time or counts events. Almost all microcontrollers include one or more timers.

## Tracepoint
A location in code at which information is logged to a trace buffer. Used for debugging without stopping execution.

## Trap
A type of exception caused by executing a special instruction.

## UClinux
A version of Linux for processors that do not have a memory management unit.

## Unidirectional
A signal that carries information in only one direction. Contrast with bidirectional.

## Unified Modeling Language
A graphical language for visualizing and documenting software systems.

## Uninitialized Data
Variables that are declared but not given an initial value. In C, uninitialized global variables are typically initialized to zero.

## Universal Serial Bus
A standard interface for connecting devices to a computer. USB supports data transfer speeds from 1.5 Mbps to 40 Gbps.

## Up Counter
A counter that increments from zero to a maximum value.

## User Mode
A processor mode in which only a subset of instructions are available. Contrast with kernel mode.

## Via
A connection between layers of a printed circuit board.

## Void
A C keyword used to indicate that a function takes no arguments or returns no value. Also used as the base type for generic pointers.

## Volatile
A C qualifier that prevents the compiler from optimizing accesses to a variable. Essential for variables that can change asynchronously, such as hardware registers.

## Volt
The SI unit of electrical potential difference.

## Voltage-Controlled Oscillator
An oscillator whose frequency is controlled by an input voltage.

## VxWorks
A commercial real-time operating system from Wind River Systems.

## Walking 1s test
A memory test that writes a pattern of 1s moving through the memory, one bit at a time.

## Watchdog Timer
A timer that must be periodically reset by software. If the software fails to reset the timer, it causes a processor reset. Used to recover from software errors.

## Watchpoint
A breakpoint that is triggered when a variable is read or written.

## Well-known Port Numbers
Port numbers from 0 to 1023 that are assigned to specific services. For example, HTTP uses port 80.

## Wind River Systems
A company that makes embedded software development tools, including the VxWorks RTOS.

## Windows CE
A real-time operating system from Microsoft for embedded systems.

## X-Acto knife
A precision cutting tool used to cut traces on a PCB during debugging.

## Xilinx
A company that makes FPGAs and CPLDs.

## XOFF
A character (Ctrl-S, ASCII 19) used in software flow control to tell the sender to pause transmission.

## XON
A character (Ctrl-Q, ASCII 17) used in software flow control to tell the sender to resume transmission.

## Y2K
Year 2000. The concern that software using two-digit years would fail when the year 2000 arrived.

## Yacc
Yet Another Compiler Compiler. A tool for generating parsers from grammar specifications.

## Yield
To voluntarily give up the processor so that other tasks can run.

## zero
In math and computing, the additive identity. Also the value used to represent false in boolean logic.

## ZIF Socket
Zero Insertion Force Socket. A socket that allows an IC to be inserted and removed without applying force.
